<h1>METAIS</h1>
<p><strong>Multi-file Extensible Templating And Instantiation Specification.</strong></p>

<p>A METAIS package is a document template in one file, usually named
<code>&lt;name&gt;-&lt;version&gt;.metais</code>. It says which inputs a
document needs, with a JSON Schema and a prose explanation for each one, and
it carries the Liquid templates that render the document from them. One
package can produce anything from a Markdown report to a set of files for a
static site.</p>

<p><code>metais</code> is the reference tool. It reads a package in place,
without unpacking it, checks the data you supply against the package's
schemas and reports every problem at once, and renders the outputs. It never
runs code from a package and never touches the network, and the same package
and data give the same bytes on every platform. It also helps authors write,
check, test and pack their own packages.</p>

<h2>Where to go next</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Someone gave you a <code>.metais</code> file:</strong>
<a href="/install/">install metais</a>, then follow
<a href="/getting-started/#use-a-package">Use a package</a>.</li>
<li><strong>You want to write a package:</strong>
<a href="/install/">install metais</a>, then follow
<a href="/getting-started/#write-a-package">Write a package</a>.</li>
<li><strong>You are building another implementation:</strong> the
specification's JSON Schemas are published at their <code>$id</code>s under
<a href="/schemas/1.0/manifest.schema.json"><code>https://metais.org/schemas/</code></a>.</li>
</ul>
